# The facility: the numbers everything else is measured from.
#
# This file is the agreement. Every other fragment includes it and derives from it, and nothing
# else in the building may invent a dimension. That is not tidiness — it is the whole of what
# makes a classical building look classical. A neoclassical facade is not a collection of
# pleasing parts; it is one set of proportions repeated at every scale, and the eye catches a
# part that was sized by taste rather than by the module long before it can say why.
#
# The order is Ionic. Its rule is the module: half the diameter of a column at its foot. Every
# other dimension in the building is some whole or simple fraction of that, and the numbers
# below are that arithmetic done once, here, so that nobody does it again differently.
#
#   diameter  D = 0.90 m          the column at its foot
#   module    M = 0.45 m          half of it — the unit everything is counted in
#
#   column    9 D = 8.10 m        base 0.5 D, shaft 8 D, capital 0.5 D (Ionic is the slender one)
#   spacing   3 D = 2.70 m        axis to axis: eustyle, the spacing Vitruvius calls the best
#   entabl.   0.25 × column       architrave, frieze, cornice — 2.02 m, taken as 2.00
#   pediment  span / 9            the low classical gable, never the steep one
#
# # Levels
#
# Everything vertical is one of these. A part that needs a height nobody else needs should still
# reach it by adding to one of these rather than by writing a number.
#
#   ground        0.00    the lawn, the datum for everything
#   podium_top    1.80    the main floor: ten risers of 0.18 up from the ground
#   floor_one     1.80    the piano nobile — the same thing said the other way
#   column_foot   1.80
#   column_head   9.90    1.80 + 8.10
#   entab_top    11.90    the main cornice, the line the whole building is capped by
#   parapet_top  13.00    the balustrade above the cornice
#   drum_top     15.60    the drum of the dome
#   dome_top     18.20
#   lantern_top  19.60
#
# # Plan
#
# North is +z, east is +x. The building faces SOUTH, so the portico and the great steps are at
# negative z and that is the elevation everything is judged from.
#
#   main block     x -16.0 .. 16.0     z  -7.5 ..  7.5      32.0 x 15.0 m
#   portico        x  -7.65 .. 7.65    z -12.5 .. -7.5      hexastyle, six columns, 5.0 m deep
#   great steps    x  -9.45 .. 9.45    z -15.7 .. -12.5     ten risers of 0.18, run 0.32
#   east wing      x   9.0 .. 16.0     the block either side of the centre
#   west wing      x -16.0 .. -9.0
#   rotunda        centred on the origin in x, on z = 0, inner radius 4.80
#
# The column axes of the portico stand at x = -6.75, -4.05, -1.35, 1.35, 4.05, 6.75. Those six
# numbers are the spine of the whole south elevation: every window, every pilaster, every step
# in the entablature above lines up with one of them or with the middle between two.

# The sampled box, and why it is no longer cut to one building.
#
# UNTIL 2026-08-17 THIS BOX WAS 34 x 20.9 x 25 m — the main block plus a metre — and the comment
# here argued at length that it had to be. The argument was sound and its conclusion was wrong,
# and both halves are worth keeping because the wrong half is the instructive one.
#
# What it said: a clip is a DENSE array over its whole bounds, four bytes a cell plus one for the
# mask, whether or not there is anything there. True, and unchanged: see `clip.voxels.assign` in
# src/forge/sample.cpp. 34 x 20.9 x 25 m at metre 32 is 582 million cells and about 2.8 GB before
# a single voxel of stone exists, so a generous margin of lawn would have doubled that for grass.
# All true. `clips/estate/BRIEF.md` reasoned from the same place to the conclusion that an estate
# a hundred metres across can only ever be a FAMILY of clips, and `colonnade.clip` says in capitals
# that it must never be added to this one.
#
# WHAT IT MISSED IS THAT THE GAME NEVER SAMPLES THE WHOLE CLIP AT metre 32. `--clip-coarse`
# defaults to 4: the up-front build samples at metre 8 and scales 4x on paste, and every node after
# that is sharpened INDIVIDUALLY, each one a `forge::sample` over its own small box with its own
# settings (src/app/main.cpp, the refine worker). The dense array over these bounds is only ever
# allocated at the COARSE metre. High detail is paid for per node, in boxes a few metres across,
# and only where something is being looked at.
#
# So the three numbers that decide whether this is a scene are these, and they are arithmetic and
# not opinion:
#
#     125.5 x 37.5 x 110.5 m  =  520,092 m3
#
#     metre  8   (what the game allocates)      266 M cells      1.33 GB     a scene
#     metre 16                                 2,130 M cells    10.6  GB     only on a big machine
#     metre 32   (whole-clip, tooling only)   17,040 M cells    85.2  GB     not on any machine
#
# THE CONSEQUENCE, AND IT IS A REAL COST: nothing can measure this clip whole at metre 32 any
# more. `clipcheck --metre 32` over these bounds asks for 85 GB and will not run. That is why
# every building in the complex carries its own probe in `requests/`, with its OWN tight bounds
# around its OWN position, and why those probes are now the only way to check a part at the
# resolution it is authored for. Whole-clip checks run at metre 8. This is not a formality: it is
# the resolution rule that half the faults in this repository hid behind, and it now has to be
# obeyed one building at a time instead of all at once.
#
# The box is cut to the complex plus about a metre. It is mostly air — the buildings occupy
# perhaps a fifth of it — and that is the price of an estate in one array, paid deliberately.
metre 32
# The east bound was 72.5 and had to move with the orangery, which was overlapping the main block.
#
# The orangery is 57.60 m of bays and its terrace slab is 59.40, and it used to be placed so that
# slab began at x 12.60 -- while the main block reaches x 16.50. So very nearly four metres of it,
# and about one whole bay of the building itself, stood INSIDE the main building's south-east
# corner rooms. Reported from playing: *"it overlaps on the opposite side from the chapel, on the
# front of the building corner on the rooms there it clips into the main building"*, and the chapel
# is at x -15.5..-6.5, so the opposite side is east and the corner is exactly this one.
#
# It is moved five metres east, which clears the main block by 1.10 m, and its east end therefore
# reaches x 77.00 against the 72.00 it used to. Matter outside the bounds is never sampled rather
# than clipped -- so an east bound left at 72.5 would have answered the overlap by slicing four
# metres off the far end of the building instead, with a flat face and no error anywhere.
bounds -53 -2 -46   78.0 35.5 64.5

# --- the site plan: where every building in the complex stands ----------------------------------
#
# North is +z, east is +x, the facility faces SOUTH, and its ground is y = 0. Every building on
# this estate was authored as a standalone clip in ITS OWN coordinates with its own origin at a
# sensible point of itself, because a tight box round a building at the origin is far cheaper than
# the same building sixty metres away with sixty metres of empty array in front of it. Folding
# them into one clip is therefore a TRANSLATION per fragment, and this table is the one place the
# vectors are written down.
#
#     fragment        translate by            occupies (facility coordinates)
#     part_theatre    -36,  0   , +8          x -52.2 .. -19.8   z  -8.2 .. 17.9
#     part_grotto     -34,  0   , +22         x -45.0 .. -23.0   z  20.5 .. 34.5
#     part_colonnade    0,  0   , -29         x -26.6 ..  26.6   z -45.7 ..-14.2
#     part_fountain     0,  0   , +26.55      x -14.4 ..  14.4   z  16.2 .. 38.3
#     part_pavilion     0, -0.90, +52         x -14.1 ..  14.1   z  40.2 .. 63.9
#     part_campanile  +30,  0   , +14         x  24.5 ..  35.5   z   8.5 .. 19.5
#     part_orangery   +42.30, +0.90, -5.25    x  12.6 ..  72.0   z -12.0 .. -0.2
#
# THREE OF THOSE ARE NOT WHAT THE BUILDING'S OWN HEADER SAYS, and each was moved for a collision
# that only appears once they are in one frame:
#
#   the theatre  was (-30, 0, +16). 32.4 m wide, it reached x -13.8 and drove through the west
#                wing, which runs out to x -16; and at z +16 it ran into the grotto. Now 2.8 m
#                clear of the facility and 2.6 m clear of the grotto.
#   the grotto   was (-34, 0, +20), moved 2 m north for the same collision, from the other side.
#   the pavilion was (0, -0.90, +46). The fountain court reaches z 38.25 and the pavilion's basin
#                began at 40.15 — clear, but only just, and two sheets of water 1.9 m apart on the
#                same axis read as one pool. At +52 they are two.
#
# The dy on the pavilion is not a collision fix and must not be "tidied": its floor sits at y 0.90
# in its own coordinates and has to land on the facility's ground at y 0.00, which sinks its basin
# 0.90 into the garden. That is what a miroir d'eau is.
#
# A TRANSLATE MOVES THE STONE AND LEAVES THE PAINT BEHIND. Every fragment here is placed by a
# transform, so every one of them must translate the shape named in each of its own
# `paint ... where=` rules by the SAME vector, and raise `below=` to 0.035 per BRIEF.md rule 5.
# The failure is silent — right geometry, wrong colours, no error, nothing in `components` — and
# the long comment at `origin` in clips/facility.clip is the same fault found the expensive way.

# --- the order, as dials -------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Written as parameters and not as bare numbers so the whole building can be re-proportioned by
# moving one of them — which is the test of whether it really was built to a module.

param D            0.90
param M            0.45
param bay          2.70
param col_h        8.10
param entab_h      2.00

param ground       0.00
param podium_top   1.80
param column_head  9.90
param entab_top   11.90
param parapet_top 13.00
param drum_top    15.60
param dome_top    18.20
param lantern_top 19.60

param block_x     16.00
param block_z      7.50
param portico_x    7.65
param portico_z   12.50
param steps_z     15.70

# --- the materials ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# A stone building is not one stone. It is a plinth of something hard and dark, a wall of
# something pale and soft enough to carve, columns of something better still, and metal where
# the hand touches it. The list is long on purpose: the renderer is being tested as much as the
# building is, and a path tracer that only ever sees grey concrete is not being asked anything.

material granite    rgb=126,124,120 rough=225
material limestone  rgb=208,203,190 rough=205
material marble     rgb=228,225,217 rough=140 translucent=110
material marble_hot rgb=232,228,214 rough=120 translucent=110
material stucco     rgb=214,206,188 rough=230
material plaster    rgb=226,222,212 rough=238
material sandstone  rgb=196,178,146 rough=215

material bronze     rgb=112,88,52   rough=110 metal=190 brush=2
material gilt       rgb=204,164,84  rough=64  metal=225
material lead       rgb=118,120,124 rough=170 metal=140 brush=2
material copper     rgb=96,140,124  rough=165 metal=110
material slate      rgb=70,72,78    rough=190

material oak        rgb=104,74,46   rough=195
material walnut     rgb=72,50,34    rough=180

material porphyry   rgb=118,52,54   rough=175
material lapis      rgb=44,64,116   rough=175
material verde      rgb=62,92,74    rough=180
material terracotta rgb=168,98,72   rough=220

material lawn       rgb=94,124,66   rough=248
material hedge      rgb=68,96,54    rough=250
material gravel     rgb=178,172,158 rough=250

material glass      rgb=198,214,224 rough=30  opacity=64  ior=1.5
material water      rgb=68,108,132  rough=24  opacity=110 ior=1.33
material lamp       rgb=255,238,206 rough=140 emit=200
material sconce     rgb=255,224,178 rough=140 emit=150

# --- the state rooms: what a baroque, rococo or renaissance interior is made of ----------------
#
# The list above is a STONE BUILDING SEEN FROM OUTSIDE, and it is complete for that. It is not
# complete for a room. A neoclassical shell is limestone, marble and bronze because that is what
# weather falls on; behind the same shell a state room of the same century is gilt on gesso, wax,
# silvered glass, lacquer, silk, porcelain and cut crystal, and NOT ONE OF THOSE IS ANYWHERE IN
# THE LIST ABOVE. Every one of them is also a distinct question for the path tracer, which is why
# they earn their place here rather than being three more greys:
#
#   `mirror`      a metal at roughness 6. Two of them facing each other across a room is the
#                 oldest hard case in the subject: a path that never terminates, an image of an
#                 image of an image, and a bounce budget that has to be spent rather than
#                 exhausted. Nothing else in this building asks for it.
#   `crystal`     ior 1.62 and nearly clear. A chandelier is four hundred small refracting solids
#                 hanging in front of a light — dispersion, caustics on the floor beneath, and a
#                 shape whose every facet is a separate refraction event.
#   `alabaster`   translucent=210. Stone you can see INTO: a lamp behind an alabaster panel is
#                 the subsurface case, and marble's translucent=110 is only half of it.
#   `porcelain`   translucency AND a clearcoat, which is the combination a glazed white body
#                 actually is and the one a single-lobe shader cannot fake.
#   `velvet`      rough=252 with sheen. The retro-reflective end of the range: a fabric that is
#                 brightest where you look along the light, and the opposite failure mode from
#                 the mirror in every way.
#   `ormolu`      fire-gilt bronze at rough=48 beside `gilt` at rough=64 and `gold_leaf` at 40 —
#                 three golds a stop apart, so "is the metal right" stops being a yes/no about
#                 one sample and becomes a comparison the eye can make inside one frame.
#
# Coloured stone earns its place differently: a room floored in `rosso` under a white vault is
# the colour-bleed rig the rotunda already runs, in five more colours, at a smaller scale where a
# second bounce that is merely bright rather than bright AND RED is obvious.

material ormolu     rgb=214,178,96  rough=48  metal=235
material gold_leaf  rgb=222,182,92  rough=40  metal=240 sheen=6
material silver     rgb=226,228,230 rough=36  metal=245
material mirror     rgb=238,242,244 rough=6   metal=252
material iron       rgb=54,54,58    rough=150 metal=180 brush=2
material steel      rgb=176,178,184 rough=90  metal=210 brush=1

material alabaster  rgb=236,230,214 rough=110 translucent=210
material onyx       rgb=206,186,150 rough=90  translucent=160
material travertine rgb=214,196,164 rough=228
material giallo     rgb=196,152,84  rough=150
material rosso      rgb=124,44,42   rough=160
material malachite  rgb=42,118,86   rough=120
material breccia    rgb=168,124,110 rough=170
material jasper     rgb=142,58,48   rough=130
material tuff       rgb=150,142,124 rough=250

material ebony      rgb=28,24,22    rough=120 lacquer=8
material boiserie   rgb=232,228,216 rough=190 lacquer=6
material lacquer_red rgb=132,26,24  rough=48  lacquer=14
material parquet    rgb=126,88,50   rough=140 lacquer=10
material gesso      rgb=238,234,224 rough=200

material damask     rgb=118,32,44   rough=246 sheen=10
material velvet     rgb=58,22,58    rough=252 sheen=14
material silk       rgb=196,176,206 rough=200 sheen=15

material porcelain  rgb=244,244,240 rough=44  translucent=90 lacquer=12
material faience    rgb=222,228,236 rough=60  lacquer=10
material shellwork  rgb=234,224,206 rough=90  sheen=12
material wax        rgb=240,232,214 rough=180 translucent=150

# The glasses, and `absorb` is the whole point of the three coloured ones: Beer-Lambert per metre,
# so a pane that is 12 mm thick is a tint and the same glass 300 mm deep is a colour. A stained
# window made of `rgb` alone is a coloured surface; made of `absorb` it is a coloured VOLUME, and
# the light that comes through it onto the floor behind carries the colour with it.
material crystal    rgb=246,248,252 rough=8   opacity=40  ior=1.62
material glass_ruby rgb=224,150,150 rough=26  opacity=70  ior=1.52 absorb=20,190,180
material glass_blue rgb=150,180,224 rough=26  opacity=70  ior=1.52 absorb=180,120,20
material glass_gold rgb=228,200,140 rough=26  opacity=70  ior=1.52 absorb=30,90,200

material candle     rgb=255,214,150 rough=150 emit=170
material taper      rgb=255,196,120 rough=150 emit=210

material boxwood    rgb=58,86,48    rough=252
material citrus     rgb=44,84,44    rough=248
material orange     rgb=224,124,36  rough=210
material bark       rgb=88,70,54    rough=246

# --- shared patterns -------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Named here so that two parts of one wall are weathered by the same grain rather than by two
# different ones, which is a seam the eye finds immediately.

let grain_fine   = fbm size=0.07 octaves=3 seed=23
let grain_broad  = fbm size=0.90 octaves=4 seed=41
let grain_stone  = fbm size=0.35 octaves=4 seed=57
let courses      = stripes axis=y period=0.60 duty=0.06
let ashlar       = bricks length=1.20 height=0.60 mortar=0.02 facing=z
let flutes_deep  = sine axis=x period=0.10 phase=0.0

# --- what this building is for ----------------------------------------------------------------
#
# It is a building, and it is also the only scene the engine is ever judged against, and the
# second of those is the reason it exists. Every fragment carries a share of that: not "make
# something that looks classical" but "make something classical that also puts a named part of
# the engine under load". The shares are written into each fragment's brief.
#
# What has to end up exercised, between all of them:
#
#   storage      uniform bricks (the podium's mass), palette bricks (a plain wall), and Direct
#                bricks where every voxel differs (variation, everywhere)
#   thin matter  features one and two voxels across — glazing bars, flutes, dentils, balusters.
#                These are what break a mesher, a sampler and a denoiser, in that order.
#   deep hollow  interiors reached only through small openings: the rotunda lit by an oculus and
#                a ring of drum windows is the hardest indirect-light problem in the building
#   materials    rough stone, polished marble, brushed and polished metal, coloured stone,
#                glass with an index of refraction, water, and emissives — all of them, in one
#                frame, so they can be compared against each other rather than in isolation
#   light        hard sun shadow across a fluted shaft; the soft gradient inside a coffer and a
#                niche; a shaft through the oculus; bounce off a coloured floor onto a white
#                vault; an emissive sconce as the only light in a closed hall
#   surface      the no-two-voxels-alike rule, and the weathering system — this is a STONE
#                building outdoors, which is the subject weathering was written for
#   walkable     a person can enter, climb, cross, and climb again without stopping
